        "original_id": "7477b72b-8ac3-5c27-8930-5af17a2d7d1d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: \"Friends in Low Places\", \"No Fences\", \"Saturday Night Special\", \"Mr. Blue\", \"I'm Gonna Hire a Wino to Decorate Our Home\"\n- People: Garth Brooks, Dewayne Blackwell, Larry Bastian, Conway Twitty, David Frizzell\n- Organizations: Billboard\n- Topics: country music, songwriters, album releases, chart performance\n\nFacts:\n- \"Mr. Blue\" was a 1959 hit song written by Dewayne Blackwell and recorded by the Fleetwoods.\n- \"I'm Gonna Hire a Wino to Decorate Our Home\" was a 1982 hit song written by Dewayne Blackwell and recorded by David Frizzell.\n- Garth Brooks performed \"Friends in Low Places,\" a country pop song released on August 6, 1990 as the lead single from the album \"No Fences.\"\n- Dewayne Blackwell wrote \"Friends in Low Places,\" which became a 1990 hit for Garth Brooks.\n- The album \"No Fences\" was released on August 27, 1990 and reached number 1 on Billboard's Top Country Albums chart.\n- Larry Bastian and Dewayne Blackwell wrote \"Saturday Night Special,\" and Conway Twitty recorded the song.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "79c4a80f-3ff0-50f2-90a2-4a41dcfa679b",
        "text": "Page: Friends in Low Places. Sentence: \"Friends in Low Places\" is a song performed by American country pop artist Garth Brooks.\nPage: Friends in Low Places. Sentence: It was released on August 6, 1990 as the lead single from his album \"No Fences\".\nPage: No Fences. Sentence: It was released on August 27, 1990, and reached #1 on \"Billboard's\" Top Country Albums chart.\nPage: Dewayne Blackwell. Sentence: His songs include \"Mr. Blue\", a 1959 hit for the Fleetwoods; \"I'm Gonna Hire a Wino to Decorate Our Home\", a 1982 hit for David Frizzell; and \"Friends in Low Places\", a 1990 hit for Garth Brooks.\nPage: Saturday Night Special (Conway Twitty song). Sentence: \"Saturday Night Special\" is a song written by Larry Bastian and Dewayne Blackwell, and recorded by American country music artist Conway Twitty.",
